              "text": "Welcome! I’d love to help you plan. A few quick questions to tailor it:\n- When are you visiting and how long will you stay?\n- What are your interests (history, art, food, shopping, nightlife, parks)?\n- Is your budget comfortable for paid attractions, or do you prefer free museums and self-guided days?\n- Are you traveling solo or with others?\n\nIn the meantime, here’s a concise starter plan to get you imagining London highlights.\n\nEasy 3-day classic itinerary\n- Day 1: Westminster & South Bank\n  - Buckingham Palace and St James’s Park\n  - Houses of Parliament, Big Ben, Westminster Abbey\n  - Walk along Whitehall to Trafalgar Square\n  - Optional: National Gallery or a ride on the London Eye\n  - Evening in Covent Garden or along the South Bank (bite to eat, river views)\n\n- Day 2: Museums & the South Bank vibe\n  - Choose one: British Museum (Bloomsbury) or Victoria and Albert Museum (South Kensington)\n  - Afternoon: Tate Modern or Shakespeare’s Globe, Millennium Bridge\n  - Optional: Borough Market for food + a stroll along the Thames\n\n- Day 3: Historic East or Greenwich\n  - Tower of London and Tower Bridge (outer views are great)\n  - If you love markets/streets, go to Shoreditch/Brick Lane for street art and bites\n  - Alternatively, take a riverboat to Greenwich: Royal Observatory and Maritime Museum, or Charles Greenwich Park\n\nTransport tips\n- Get around with an Oyster card or contactless payment; Zone 1–2 covers most sights.\n- Walking is often quicker than short Tube rides in central areas.\n- Consider a 2- to 3-day Travelcard if you’ll use Tube/bus a lot.\n- River services ( Thames Clippers) are a scenic travel option between central spots.\n\nMust-know tips\n- Free essentials: many museums are free (British Museum, National Gallery, Tate Britain, Victoria and Albert), with paid special exhibitions.\n- Book in advance for popular attractions (Tower of London, London Eye, West End shows).\n- Weather: London is notorious for variable rain—carry a compact umbrella and layers.\n- Power: UK plugs are type G; bring a small adapter if needed.\n- Food: try a Sunday roast, fish and chips, and coffee culture around Notting Hill, Covent Garden, or Shoreditch.\n\nWould you like me to tailor this to your exact dates, interests, and pace? I can also fetch the current London weather and give you a day-by-day plan with times and restaurant suggestions.",
